window.KB_BODY=window.KB_BODY||{};
window.KB_BODY[14]={"20-018-016":"<h3>Specifications</h3><p>with Electronically Actuated Injector</p><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>For performance and fuel rate values, refer to the Engine Data Sheet or the fuel pump code for the particular rating involved.</p></div></div><h4>Engine Idle Speed</h4><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Engine Idle Speed</th><th>See Engine Data Sheet</th></tr></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div><h4>Fuel Inlet Maximum Restriction</h4><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Dirty Fuel Filter</th><th>34kPa [10 in-Hg]</th></tr></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div><h4>Fuel Drain Line Restriction:</h4><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>With Check Valves</th><th>127 mm-Hg [5 in-Hg]</th></tr></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>With Check Valves Removed</th><th>254 mm-Hg [10 in-Hg]</th></tr></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div><h4>Water Removal:</h4><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Free</th><th>95 percent</th></tr></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Emulsified</th><th>95 percent</th></tr></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Engine Minimum Cranking Speed</th><th>150 rpm</th></tr></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div><p>with Mechanically Actuated Injector</p><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>For performance and fuel rate values, refer to the Engine Data Sheet or the fuel pump code for the particular rating involved.</p></div></div><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Engine Idle Speed</th><th>600 to 1400 rpm</th></tr></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div><h4>Fuel Inlet Maximum Restriction:</h4><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Clean Fuel Filter</th><th>100 mm-Hg [4.0 in-Hg]</th></tr></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Dirty Fuel Filter</th><th>203 mm-Hg [8.0 in-Hg]</th></tr></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div><h4>Fuel Drain Line Restriction:</h4><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>With Check Valves</th><th>0 to 21 kPa [0 to 3 psi]</th></tr></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>With Check Valves Removed</th><th>14 to 34 kPa [2 to 5 psi]</th></tr></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div><h4>Fuel Check Valve Between Fuel Pump and Cylinder Head (integral to fuel pump):</h4><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Opening Pressure</th><th>21 to 35 kPa [3 to 5 psi]</th></tr></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Engine Minimum Cranking Speed</th><th>150 rpm</th></tr></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div><h4>Fuel Check Valve in Fuel Drain Line:</h4><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Opening Pressure</th><th>13 to 25 mm-Hg [1/4 to 1/2 psi]</th></tr></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Derate Engine Fuel Rate for High Altitude</th><th>4% per 300 m [1000 ft] above 3600 m [12,000 ft]</th></tr></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Derate Engine Fuel Rate for Hot Weather</th><th>2% per 11°C above 38°C [1% per 10°F above 100°F]</th></tr></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Shutoff Valve Solenoid Coil Resistance in ohms 24 VDC</th><th>28 to 32 ohms</th></tr></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Fuel Pump Cranking Pressure - Minimum</th><th>172 kPa [25 psi] at 150 rpm</th></tr></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div><h4>Fuel Pump Pressure - Minimum (on engine measurement):</h4><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>1500 rpm</th><th>1448 kPa [210 psi]</th></tr></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>1800 rpm</th><th>1655 kPa [240 psi]</th></tr></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>2100 rpm</th><th>1861 kPa [270 psi]</th></tr></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>2350 rpm</th><th>1999 kPa [290 psi]</th></tr></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>","28-000-001-tr":"<h3>Preparatory Steps</h3><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Batteries can emit explosive gases. To reduce the possibility of personal injury, always ventilate the compartment before servicing the batteries. To reduce the possibility of arcing, remove the negative (-) battery cable first and attach the negative (-) battery cable last.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Coolant is toxic. Keep away from small children and pets. If not reused, dispose of in accordance with local environmental regulations.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Do not remove the pressure cap from a hot engine. Wait until the coolant temperature is below 50°C [120°F] before removing the pressure cap. Heated coolant spray or steam can cause personal injury.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>To reduce the possibility of personal injury, avoid direct contact of hot oil with your skin.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Some state and federal agencies have determined that used engine oil can be carcinogenic and cause reproductive toxicity. Avoid inhalation of vapors, ingestion, and prolonged contact with used engine oil. If not reused dispose of in accordance with local environmental regulations.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>This component or assembly weighs greater than 23 kg [50 lb]. To prevent serious personal injury, be sure to have assistance or use appropriate lifting equipment to lift this component or assembly.</p></div></div><ul><li>Attach tags to all hoses, lines, linkages, and electrical connections, as they are removed, to identify location and aid the installation process.</li><li>Disconnect the battery cables. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/99-013-009\">Refer to Procedure 013-009 (Battery Cables and Connections) in Section 13.</a></li><li>Drain the engine coolant. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-008-018-tr\">Refer to Procedure 008-018 (Cooling System) in Section 8.</a></li><li>Drain the lubricating oil. Refer to Procedure 007-037 (Lubricating Oil System) in Section 7.</li></ul><ul><li>Attach tags to all hoses, lines, linkages, and electrical connections, as they are removed, to identify location and aid the installation process.</li><li>Disconnect the batteries. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/99-013-009\">Refer to Procedure 013-009 (Battery Cables and Connections) in Section 13.</a></li><li>Disconnect the air/fuel supply to the air starter if equipped. Refer to Procedure 012-022 (Air Starting Motor) in Section 12.</li><li>Drain the engine coolant. Refer to Procedure 008-018 (Cooling System) in Section 8.</li><li>Drain the lubricating oil from the engine. Refer to Procedure 007-037 (Lubricating Oil System) in Section 7.</li><li>Disconnect the starter cable, engine ground straps, cab or chassis to engine hoses, tubing, electrical wires, and hydraulic lines.</li><li>Disconnect all hoses, tubing, electrical wires, and hydraulic lines, if equipped.</li><li>Disconnect the intake and exhaust system pipes. Refer to the OEM installation instructions.</li><li>Disconnect the drive units from the flywheel housing and flywheel. Refer to the OEM service manual.</li><li>Remove air conditioning lines, if needed. Refer to the OEM service manual.</li><li>Remove all chassis components necessary to remove the engine from the equipment. Refer to the OEM service manual.</li></ul><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ck800wa.png\" alt=\"ck800wa.png\"></figure><h3>Remove</h3><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>The engine illustrations in this procedure could be different for a specific application, but the removal steps are the same regardless of engine type.</p></div></div><p>Disconnect the starter cable, engine ground straps, cab or chassis to engine hoses, tubing, electrical wires, and hydraulic lines.</p><p>Disconnect all hoses, tubing, electrical wires, and hydraulic lines, if equipped.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/13200042.png\" alt=\"13200042.png\"></figure><p>Disconnect the intake and exhaust system pipes.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/oi400hc.png\" alt=\"oi400hc.png\"></figure><p>Disconnect the drive units from the flywheel housing and flywheel.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/tr400wa.png\" alt=\"tr400wa.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>If a liquid refrigerant system (air conditioning) is used, wear eye and face protection, and wrap a cloth around the fittings before removing. Liquid refrigerant can cause serious eye and skin injuries.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>To protect the environment, liquid refrigerant systems must be properly emptied and filled using equipment that prevents the release of refrigerant gas into the atmosphere. Federal law requires capturing and recycling the refrigerant.</p></div></div><p>Remove air conditioning lines if needed.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/oi800qb.png\" alt=\"oi800qb.png\"></figure><p>Remove all chassis components necessary to remove the engine from the equipment.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/00600043.png\" alt=\"00600043.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>This component or assembly weighs greater than 23 kg [50 lb]. To prevent serious personal injury, be sure to have assistance or use appropriate lifting equipment to lift this component or assembly.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Before lifting the engine, check the four engine lifting brackets. Be sure each bracket contains a spot-face under the hardened flat washer. if the bracket is not spot-faced, remove the capscrew. Check for rust and corrosion. If the capscrew has rust or corrosion, replace the capscrew before lifting the engine. Refer to Procedure 016-001 (Engine Lifting Brackets) in Section 16. Refer to Procedure 016-001 (Engine Lifting Brackets) in Section 16.</p></div></div><p>KTA38GC, K38 and QSK38 engines have four lifting brackets, two on each bank. The lifting brackets for the K38 are installed between cylinder numbers 1 and 2, and 5 and 6.</p><p>The lifting brackets for the K50 and QSK50 are installed between cylinder numbers 2 and 3, and 6 and 7.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ew600li.png\" alt=\"ew600li.png\"></figure><p>Remove the lifting brackets. Check for cracks, rust and corrosion. Install lifting brackets. Refer to Procedure 016-001 (Engine Lifting Brackets) in Section 16. Refer to Procedure 016-001 (Engine Lifting Brackets) in Section 16.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/16600023.png\" alt=\"16600023.png\"></figure><p>The engine lifting fixtures, Part Number 3163264, are designed to lift the engine <b>only</b>. Any additional components attached (including to the drive end, non-drive end and/or chassis) <b>must</b> be removed.</p><p>The lifting force <b>must</b> be in a straight vertical line with the lifting brackets in both directions.</p><p>Engine lifting fixture, Part Number 3163264, is available from Cummins Inc. This fixture is available to lift the KTA38GC, K38, K50, QSK38, QSK50 assembled engines.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/00600238.png\" alt=\"00600238.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout warning\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>CAUTION · Осторожно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Do not attempt to lift the engine with an apparatus that allows side-loading of the lifting brackets, or allows the chain or cable to apply force to other parts of the engine. The lifting brackets or parts can break, causing the engine to fall and be damaged.</p></div></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/00600239.png\" alt=\"00600239.png\"></figure><p><b>Always</b> use the lifting brackets that are connected to the base rails when drive units or a radiator is mounted on the base rails with the engine.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/00600240.png\" alt=\"00600240.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout warning\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>CAUTION · Осторожно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Never use the engine lifting brackets or the engine lifting fixture, Part Number 3163264, to lift an engine when drive units or a radiator is mounted on common base rails. Never use the lifting bracket on the block to lift an engine that is mounted on base rails when there is a driven or radiator also mounted on the base rails. The lifting brackets or parts can break, causing the engine to fall. Engine damage can result.</p></div></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ew600lf.png\" alt=\"ew600lf.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout warning\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>CAUTION · Осторожно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Do not attempt to lift the engine with an apparatus that allows side-loading of the lifting brackets, or allows the chain or cable to apply force to other parts of the engine. The lifting brackets or parts can break, causing the engine to fall and be damaged.</p></div></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/00600241.png\" alt=\"00600241.png\"></figure><h3>Finishing Steps</h3><p>Cover all engine openings to prevent dirt and debris from entering the engine.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/tb4otla.png\" alt=\"tb4otla.png\"></figure><p>Remove all remaining accessories and brackets that will be used on the replacement engine.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ew400hc.png\" alt=\"ew400hc.png\"></figure>","28-000-002-tr":"<h3>Preparatory Steps</h3><p>Install all accessories and brackets that were removed from the engine.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ew400hc.png\" alt=\"ew400hc.png\"></figure><p>Uncover all the engine openings.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/tb4otla.png\" alt=\"tb4otla.png\"></figure><h3>Install</h3><div class=\"callout warning\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>CAUTION · Осторожно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Never use the engine lifting brackets or the engine lifting fixture, Part Number 3163264, to lift an engine when drive units or a radiator is mounted on common base rails. Never use the lifting bracket on the block to lift an engine that is mounted on base rails when there is a drive or radiator also mounted on the base rails. The lifting brackets or parts can break, causing the engine to fall. Engine damage can result.</p></div></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ew600lf.png\" alt=\"ew600lf.png\"></figure><p><b>Always</b> use the lifting brackets that are connected to the base rails when drive units or a radiator is mounted on the base rails with the engine.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/00600240.png\" alt=\"00600240.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout warning\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>CAUTION · Осторожно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Do not attempt to lift the engine with an apparatus that allows side-loading of the lifting brackets, or allows the chain or cable to apply force to other parts of the engine. The lifting brackets or parts can break, causing the engine to fall and be damaged.</p></div></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/00600239.png\" alt=\"00600239.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout warning\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>CAUTION · Осторожно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Before lifting the engine, check the four engine lifting brackets. Be sure each bracket contains a spot-face under the hardened flat washer. if the bracket is not spot-faced, remove the capscrew. Check for rust and corrosion. If the capscrew has rust or corrosion, replace the capscrew before lifting the engine.</p></div></div><p>KTA38GC, K38 and QSK38 engines have four lifting brackets, two on each bank. The lifting brackets are installed between cylinder numbers 1 and 2, and 5 and 6.</p><p>The lifting brackets for the K50 are installed between cylinder numbers 2 and 3, and 6 and 7.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ew600li.png\" alt=\"ew600li.png\"></figure><p>Remove the lifting brackets. Check for cracks, rust and corrosion. Install lifting brackets. Refer to Procedure 016-001 (Engine Lifting Brackets) in Section 16.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/16600023.png\" alt=\"16600023.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>This component or assembly weighs greater than 23 kg [50 lb]. To prevent serious personal injury, be sure to have assistance or use appropriate lifting equipment to lift this component or assembly.</p></div></div><p>The engine lifting fixtures, Part Number 3163264, are designed to lift the engine <b>only</b>. Any additional components attached (including to the drive end, non-drive end and/or chassis) <b>must</b> be removed.</p><p>The lifting force <b>must</b> be in a straight vertical line with the lifting brackets in both directions.</p><p>Engine lifting fixture, Part Number 3163264, is available from Cummins Inc. This fixture is available to lift the KTA38GC, K38, K50, QSK38, QSK50 assembled engines.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/00600238.png\" alt=\"00600238.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>This component or assembly weighs greater than 23 kg [50 lb]. To prevent serious personal injury, be sure to have assistance or use appropriate lifting equipment to lift this component or assembly.</p></div></div><p>Use a properly rated hoist and Part Number 3163264 Engine Lifting Fixture, to install the engine.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/00600042.png\" alt=\"00600042.png\"></figure><p>With the engine installed, check the crankshaft end clearance to make sure it is still within specification. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-001-007-tr\">Refer to Procedure 001-007 in Section 1.</a></p><p>Remove an accessible hand hole cover. Refer to Procedure 001-035 in Section 1. The crankshaft can be moved forward and backward by using a pry bar on the crankshaft webs through the hand hole cover opening. The end clearance can be measured through the hand hole cover opening on a crankshaft counterweight or on the front damper.</p><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Do not use a pry bar on the damper to move the crankshaft. Doing so may cause damage to the damper.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>The oil pan is shown removed from the engine for illustration purposes.</p></div></div><p>Once the end clearance is confirmed to be within specification, replace the hand hole cover. Refer to Procedure 001-035 in Section 1. If the end clearance is out of specification, check for misalignment with the attached drive units.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/mb1beja.png\" alt=\"mb1beja.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Inspect the engine lifting brackets for damage or cracks. Do not attempt to lift the engine if any cracks or damage are visible. Using damaged lifting equipment can cause serious personal injury and/or property damage.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout warning\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>CAUTION · Осторожно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Make sure all lines, hoses, and tubes are in good condition, properly routed, and fastened to prevent engine damage.</p></div></div><p>Align the engine in the chassis on base rails, and tighten the engine mounting capscrews. See the OEM torque specifications.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/00600040.png\" alt=\"00600040.png\"></figure><h3>Finishing Steps</h3><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Batteries can emit explosive gases. To reduce the possibility of personal injury, always ventilate the compartment before servicing the batteries. To reduce the possibility of arcing, remove the negative (-) battery cable first and attach the negative (-) battery cable last.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Coolant is toxic. Keep away from small children and pets. If not reused, dispose of in accordance with local environmental regulations.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Do not remove the pressure cap from a hot engine. Wait until the coolant temperature is below 50°C [120°F] before removing the pressure cap. Heated coolant spray or steam can cause personal injury.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>To reduce the possibility of personal injury, avoid direct contact of hot oil with your skin.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Some state and federal agencies have determined that used engine oil can be carcinogenic and cause reproductive toxicity. Avoid inhalation of vapors, ingestion, and prolonged contact with used engine oil. If not reused dispose of in accordance with local environmental regulations.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>This component or assembly weighs greater than 23 kg [50 lb]. To prevent serious personal injury, be sure to have assistance or use appropriate lifting equipment to lift this component or assembly.</p></div></div><ul><li>Fill the engine with clean lubricating oil. Refer to Procedure 007-037 in Section 7.</li></ul><ul><li>Fill the cooling system with coolant. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-008-018-tr\">Refer to Procedure 008-018 in Section 8.</a></li><li>Connect the air supply to the air starter, if equipped. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/102-012-022\">Refer to Procedure 012-022 in Section 12.</a></li><li>Install air conditioning lines, if needed. Refer to the OEM service manual.</li><li>Connect the intake and exhaust system pipes. Refer to the OEM service manual.</li><li>Install all chassis components necessary to install the engine to the equipment. Refer to the OEM service manual.</li><li>Connect the starter cable, engine ground straps, cab, or chassis to engine hoses, tubing, electrical wires, and hydraulic lines.</li><li>Connect the battery cables. Refer to the OEM service manual.</li><li>Make final inspection to make sure all hoses, wires, linkages, and components have been properly installed and tightened.</li><li>Operate engine at low idle for 2 to 3 minutes.</li><li>Stop the engine and wait 5 to 7 minutes for the oil to drain to the oil pan. Check the oil and coolant levels again.</li><li>Fill the engine to the correct oil and coolant levels, if necessary. Refer to Procedure 007-037 in Section 7. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-008-018-tr\">Refer to Procedure 008-018 in Section 8.</a></li><li>Operate the engine for 8 to 10 minutes to check for proper operation, unusual noises, and coolant, fuel, and lubricating oil leaks. Refer to Procedure 014-006 in Section 14.</li></ul><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ck800wa.png\" alt=\"ck800wa.png\"></figure>","28-001-003-tr":"<h3>Select Service Tools</h3><h4>Recommended Cummins® Service Tools</h4><ul><li>Accessory Drive Seal Installer Kit, Part Number 5573466</li></ul><h4>Additional Service Items</h4><ul><li>Mallet</li></ul><h3>Preparatory Steps</h3><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Batteries can emit explosive gases. To reduce the possibility of personal injury, always ventilate the compartment before servicing the batteries. To reduce the possibility of arcing, remove the negative (-) battery cable first and attach the negative (-) battery cable last.</p></div></div><ul><li>Remove the pulley from the accessory drive shaft. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-009-004-tr\">Refer to Procedure 009-004 in Section 9.</a></li><li>Disconnect the batteries. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/99-013-009\">Refer to Procedure 013-009 in Section 13.</a></li><li>Disconnect the air/gas supply to the starter if equipped. Refer to Procedure 012-022 in Section 12.</li><li>Remove the pulley from the accessory drive shaft. Refer to Procedure 009-004 in Section 9.</li></ul><h3>Remove</h3><p>Use a brass drift to remove the woodruff key (3) from the shaft.</p><p>Remove the keyway seal.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/dp6puma.png\" alt=\"dp6puma.png\"></figure><p>Remove the old accessory drive shaft seal from the front gear housing cover.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600317.png\" alt=\"01600317.png\"></figure><h3>Install</h3><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>If the engine does <b>not</b> have a pulley on the accessory drive shaft, <b>only</b> a cover will be installed. Refer to Procedure 009-039 in Section 9.</p></div></div><p>Use seal installer, Part Number 5573467. Install the accessory drive seal.</p><p>Push the seal into the cover until the service tool flange stops against the gear cover.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01100186.png\" alt=\"01100186.png\"></figure><p>Install the keyway seal (4) and the accessory drive shaft woodruff key (5).</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/dp6puha.png\" alt=\"dp6puha.png\"></figure><h3>Finishing Steps</h3><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Batteries can emit explosive gases. To reduce the possibility of personal injury, always ventilate the compartment before servicing the batteries. To reduce the possibility of arcing, remove the negative (-) battery cable first and attach the negative (-) battery cable last.</p></div></div><ul><li>Install pulley onto the accessory drive shaft. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-009-004-tr\">Refer to Procedure 009-004 in Section 9</a>.</li><li>Operate the engine and check for leaks.</li><li>Install the pulley from the accessory drive shaft. Refer to Procedure 009-004 in Section 9.</li><li>Connect the air/gas supply to the starter if equipped. Refer to Procedure 012-022 in Section 12.</li><li>Connect the batteries. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/99-013-009\">Refer to Procedure 013-009 in Section 13</a>.</li><li>Operate the engine and check for leaks.</li></ul>","28-001-005-tr":"<h3>Preparatory Steps</h3><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Batteries can emit explosive gases. To reduce the possibility of personal injury, always ventilate the compartment before servicing the batteries. To reduce the possibility of arcing, remove the negative (-) battery cable first and attach the negative (-) battery cable last.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Some state and federal agencies have determined that used engine oil can be carcinogenic and cause reproductive toxicity. Avoid inhalation of vapors, ingestion, and prolonged contact with used engine oil. If not reused, dispose of in accordance with local environmental regulations.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>To reduce the possibility of personal injury, avoid direct contact of hot oil with your skin.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>This component weighs greater than 23 kg [50 lb]. To prevent serious personal injury, be sure to have assistance or use appropriate lifting equipment to lift this component or assembly.</p></div></div><ul><li>Disconnect the batteries. See equipment manufacturer service information.</li><li>Drain the lubricating oil. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-007-025-tr\">Refer to Procedure 007-025 in Section 7.</a></li><li>Remove the full flow filters. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-007-013-tr\">Refer to Procedure 007-013 in Section 7.</a></li><li>Remove the lubricating oil pan adapter assembly. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-007-017\">Refer to Procedure 007-017 in Section 7.</a></li><li>Remove the oil suction tube and brackets, if necessary, to access the connecting rod bearings. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-007-040-tr\">Refer to Procedure 007-040 in Section 7.</a></li></ul><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ck800wa.png\" alt=\"ck800wa.png\"></figure><p>Use the barring mechanism (3) to rotate the crankshaft. Position the connecting rods near the bottom center for maximum accessibility when loosening the connecting rod capscrews.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600320.png\" alt=\"01600320.png\"></figure><h3>Remove</h3><p>Loosen the capscrews until there is approximately 6.0 mm [0.24 in] of clearance between the cap and the capscrew head.</p><p>Use a soft-headed mallet to tap the capscrews alternately until the rod separates from the cap. Remove the capscrews and the cap.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600321.png\" alt=\"01600321.png\"></figure><p>Remove the lower rod bearing. Scribe the bearing position in the tang area, as shown, for reuse or analysis.</p><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Connecting rods have their cylinder number and position stamped on the side of the rod and cap that is toward the camshaft. Check the rods and caps for correct markings.</p></div></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600322.png\" alt=\"01600322.png\"></figure><p>Push the rods up far enough to allow the upper bearings to be removed. Mark the cylinder number and location in the tang area, as described, for the lower bearing.</p><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Use of connecting rod guide pins, or equivalent, to prevent journal damage is recommended.</p></div></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600323.png\" alt=\"01600323.png\"></figure><h3>Clean and Inspect for Reuse</h3><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>If bearing replacement was initiated due to malfunction, check the oil pan for debris. If significant debris is present, remove and disassemble the lube oil pump and inspect the bushings for debris damage. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-007-031-tr\">Refer to Procedure 007-031 in Section 7.</a></p></div></div><p>Clean the crankshaft journal with a lint-free cloth and inspect the journal for damage.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600324.png\" alt=\"01600324.png\"></figure><p>Clean the connecting rod and cap with a lint-free cloth. Inspect the rod cap, rod saddle, and capscrews for nicks, cracks, burrs, scratches, or fretting.</p><p>If nicks in the saddle area can <b>not</b> be removed with crocus cloth, the rod <b>must</b> be replaced.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/cx8behf.png\" alt=\"cx8behf.png\"></figure><p>The bearings <b>must</b> be marked for location as they are removed for future identification. Each bearing <b>must</b> be installed in its original location if the bearing is installed again.</p><p>Use a scribe or ball point pen to mark the bearing location in the tang area.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/cx1bema.png\" alt=\"cx1bema.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>When using solvents, acids or alkaline materials for cleaning, follow the manufacture&#x27;s recommendations for use. Wear goggles and protective clothing to avoid personal injury.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Wear appropriate eye and face protection when using compressed air. Flying debris and dirt can cause personal injury.</p></div></div><p>Use solvent to clean the bearings.</p><p>Dry with compressed air.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/cx8beea.png\" alt=\"cx8beea.png\"></figure><p>Inspect the bearing face on the front and back for:</p><ul><li>Nicks</li><li>Cracks</li><li>Burrs</li><li>Scratches (deep enough to be felt with a fingernail)</li><li>Scrapes</li><li>Gouges</li><li>Embedded particles</li><li>Fretting</li><li>Pitting</li><li>Flaking</li><li>Corrosion</li><li>Lock tang damage.</li></ul><p>If any damage is found, the bearing <b>must</b> be replaced.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/cx2besa.png\" alt=\"cx2besa.png\"></figure><p>Normal bearing wear produces a smooth finish, which will wear into the copper lining. Exposed copper does <b>not always</b> indicate worn bearings. Reference the Parts Reuse Guidelines, Bulletin 3810303.</p><p>If large areas of copper lining are visible in the bearings before the engine has accumulated 3000 hours, inspect the engine for contamination from fine dirt particles and correct the problem.</p><p>For more detailed information of bearing damage, reference the Analysis and Prevention of Bearing Failures Manual, Bulletin 3810387.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/cx8beba.png\" alt=\"cx8beba.png\"></figure><p>Use a ball-end micrometer to measure the thickness of the bearing in the wear location.</p><p>Replace the bearing when it is <b>not</b> within specifications.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Connecting Rod Bearing Thickness Standard or Oversize</th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td></td><td>mm</td><td></td><td>in</td></tr><tr><td>Standard</td><td>3.106</td><td>MIN</td><td>0.1223</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>3.157</td><td>MAX</td><td>0.1243</td></tr><tr><td>0.010 Oversize</td><td>3.233</td><td>MIN</td><td>0.1273</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>3.284</td><td>MAX</td><td>0.1293</td></tr><tr><td>0.020 Oversize</td><td>3.360</td><td>MIN</td><td>0.1323</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>3.411</td><td>MAX</td><td>0.1343</td></tr><tr><td>0.030 Oversize</td><td>3.487</td><td>MIN</td><td>0.1373</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>3.538</td><td>MAX</td><td>0.1393</td></tr><tr><td>0.040 Oversize</td><td>3.614</td><td>MIN</td><td>0.1423</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>3.665</td><td>MAX</td><td>0.1443</td></tr></tbody></table></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/mb8betb.png\" alt=\"mb8betb.png\"></figure><h3>Install</h3><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>If bearings are to be reused, make sure they are put back in their original locations.</p></div></div><p>Part numbers of upper and lower new bearings <b>must</b> be identical.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600325.png\" alt=\"01600325.png\"></figure><p>Lubricate the upper bearing with engine oil and install it in the connecting rod with the tang seated firmly in the machined slot.</p><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Rod bearings are available in oversizes of 0.010, 0.020, 0.030, and 0.040 inch. If rod journals have been ground undersize, the amount will be stamped on the counterweight closest to the front of the engine.</p></div></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600326.png\" alt=\"01600326.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout warning\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>CAUTION · Осторожно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Do not lubricate the back of the bearing shells.</p></div></div><p>Install the lower bearing shell in the rod cap. Make sure the bearing tang is in place in the machined slot. Also, make sure the bearing ends are even with the parting surfaces.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/cx8behd.png\" alt=\"cx8behd.png\"></figure><p>Lubricate the connecting rod capscrew threads and washers with engine oil and allow the excess to run off.</p><p>Install the washers and capscrews in the caps.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/cx8csha.png\" alt=\"cx8csha.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout warning\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>CAUTION · Осторожно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Connecting rods and caps are not interchangeable, and caps fit in only one direction. Rods and caps are machined as an assembly and failure will result if they are mixed.</p></div></div><p>Install the connecting rod caps. Make sure the side of the cap with the chamfer is toward the fillet of the journal.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/cx6caha.png\" alt=\"cx6caha.png\"></figure><p>Use the following steps to tighten the capscrews. Apply torque plus angle to both capscrews on the connecting rod.</p><p>Torque Value:</p><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>If it is <b>not</b> possible to use an angle gauge when applying the required torque to the connecting rod capscrews, the following sequence can be used:</p></div></div><p>Use the following steps to tighten the capscrews.</p><p>Torque Value:</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/cx6boha.png\" alt=\"cx6boha.png\"></figure><p>Loosen both of the connecting rod capscrews.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600327.png\" alt=\"01600327.png\"></figure><p>Use the connecting rod capscrew tool (1), Part Number 3822764, and the following procedure to measure the stretch of the capscrews.</p><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>The use of two tools reduces the possibility of error and requires less time. The following steps show the usage of one tool.</p></div></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/cx6toga.png\" alt=\"cx6toga.png\"></figure><p>Position the tool on the first rod capscrew as shown.</p><p>Make sure the anvil is centered in the hole of the rod capscrew and the tool contacts the head correctly.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/cx6toha.png\" alt=\"cx6toha.png\"></figure><p>Check to make sure there is a minimum of 0.030 inch upward travel remaining on the indicator.</p><p>If there is <b>not</b> sufficient travel, loosen the set screw on the bottom of the tool and move the anvil downward.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/cx6toub.png\" alt=\"cx6toub.png\"></figure><p>Make sure the tool is in position and adjust the indicator dial until the needle points to the 0.050 inch mark.</p><p>Record the value as “first bolt zero”. Tighten the lock (2) on the indicator dial to maintain the reading.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/cx6toua.png\" alt=\"cx6toua.png\"></figure><p>Remove the tool from the first reading and position it on the other capscrew of the same rod.</p><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>There is no requirement for the capscrews to be the same length.</p></div></div><p>Record the reading obtained as “second bolt zero”.</p><p>Remove the tool.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/cx6bota.png\" alt=\"cx6bota.png\"></figure><p>Tighten the capscrews alternately and evenly. Use the previous torque values in this procedure.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/cx6boha.png\" alt=\"cx6boha.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Do <b>not</b> adjust the indicator dial.</p></div></div><p>Place the tool on the first connecting rod capscrew and take the reading 1.78 mm [0.070 in].</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/cx6botb.png\" alt=\"cx6botb.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Do <b>not</b> adjust the indicator dial.</p></div></div><p>Place the tool on the second rod capscrew and take the reading.</p><p>Record the value as “second bolt tight”. In this example, the value is 1.42 mm [0.056 in].</p><p>Remove the tool.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/cx6botc.png\" alt=\"cx6botc.png\"></figure><p>Determine the amount of capscrew stretch by subtracting the “bolt zero” from the “bolt tight” value for each capscrew.</p><p>Computing Capscrew Stretch</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Computing Capscrew Stretch</th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>“First bolt tight” value</td><td>0.070 inch</td></tr><tr><td>“First bolt zero” value</td><td>0.050 inch</td></tr><tr><td>“First bolt stretch”</td><td>= 0.020 inch</td></tr><tr><td>“Second bolt tight” value</td><td>0.056 inch</td></tr><tr><td>“Second bolt zero” value</td><td>0.045 inch</td></tr><tr><td>“Second bolt stretch”</td><td>= 0.011 inch</td></tr></tbody></table></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/cx6botd.png\" alt=\"cx6botd.png\"></figure><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Rod Capscrew Stretch Specification</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>mm</td><td></td><td>in</td></tr><tr><td>0.343</td><td>MIN</td><td>0.0135</td></tr><tr><td>0.470</td><td>MAX</td><td>0.0185</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>If the capscrew stretch is greater than specification, loosen both capscrews and remove and discard the one that was greater than specification.</p><p>Install the new capscrew using the specified torque steps and repeat the capscrew stretch measurement procedure, assuming the other capscrew was within specifications.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600325.png\" alt=\"01600325.png\"></figure><p>If the capscrew stretch is less than specification, loosen both capscrews and proceed as follows:</p><p>Tighten the capscrews using the same steps and values except for the final step for the capscrew that was less than specification. Tighten this capscrew.</p><div class=\"callout tip\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">🔧</span>Момент затяжки · Torque Value</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>365 n•m [269 ft-lb]</p></div></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600329.png\" alt=\"01600329.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout warning\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>CAUTION · Осторожно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>If the capscrew that was within stretch specifications is tightened to the maximum torque value, capscrew failure can result and cause extensive engine damage.</p></div></div><p>Use the tool to measure the amount of capscrew stretch and calculate as previously.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/cx6botc.png\" alt=\"cx6botc.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>If the capscrew that was tightened further to the maximum torque amount remains less than the minimum stretch specification, the connecting rod and capscrew <b>must</b> be replaced.</p></div></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600330.png\" alt=\"01600330.png\"></figure><h4>Side Clearance Check</h4><ul><li>To measure the amount of connecting rod side clearance, move the rods as far as they will go toward the crankshaft web. Measure side clearance with a feeler gauge.</li><li><a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-001-054-tr\">Refer to Procedure 001-054 in Section 1.</a></li></ul><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Both rods <b>must</b> move freely from side-to-side.</p></div></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/cx600da.png\" alt=\"cx600da.png\"></figure><h3>Finishing Steps</h3><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Batteries can emit explosive gases. To reduce the possibility of personal injury, always ventilate the compartment before servicing the batteries. To reduce the possibility of arcing, remove the negative (-) battery cable first and attach the negative (-) battery cable last.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>This component weighs greater than 23 kg [50 lb]. To prevent serious personal injury, be sure to have assistance or use appropriate lifting equipment to lift this component or assembly.</p></div></div><ul><li>Install the oil suction tube and brackets. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-007-040-tr\">Refer to Procedure 007-040 in Section 7.</a></li><li>Install the lubricating oil pan adapter assembly. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-007-017\">Refer to Procedure 007-017 in Section 7.</a></li><li>Install new full flow filters. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-007-013-tr\">Refer to Procedure 007-013 in Section 7.</a></li><li>Fill engine with clean 15W-40 lubricating oil. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-007-025-tr\">Refer to Procedure 007-025 in Section 7.</a></li><li>Connect the batteries. See equipment manufacturer service information.</li><li>Operate the engine and check for leaks.</li></ul><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ck800wa.png\" alt=\"ck800wa.png\"></figure>","28-001-006-tr":"<h3>General Information</h3><p>The upper bearings contain an oil hole. The lower bearings do <b>not</b> have an oil hole. Both bearings are marked on the back indicating location (upper or lower) and size (standard [STD] or oversize [OS]). The amount the bearing is oversize is indicated in inches.</p><p>Use the same size bearing; standard, 0.254 mm [0.010 in], 0.508 mm [0.020 in], or 0.762 mm [0.030 in] that was removed.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600076.png\" alt=\"01600076.png\"></figure><p>The crankshaft will be stamped on the end of the number one counterweight to indicate whether it has been ground undersize. The amounts of material removed from the main journals and the rod journals are both stamped at this location. Thrust bearing size is stamped on a crankshaft counterweight adjacent to the thrust location.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ck800ga.png\" alt=\"ck800ga.png\"></figure><h3>Preparatory Steps</h3><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Some state and federal agencies have determined that used engine oil can be carcinogenic and cause reproductive toxicity. Avoid inhalation of vapors, ingestion, and prolonged contact with used engine oil. If not reused, dispose of in accordance with local environmental regulations.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>To reduce the possibility of personal injury, avoid direct contact of hot oil with your skin.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>This component or assembly weighs greater than 23 kg [50 lb]. To prevent serious personal injury, be sure to have assistance or use appropriate lifting equipment to lift this component or assembly.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Batteries can emit explosive gases. To reduce the possibility of personal injury, always ventilate the compartment before servicing the batteries. To reduce the possibility of arcing, remove the negative (-) battery cable first and attach the negative (-) battery cable last.</p></div></div><ul><li>Disconnect the batteries. Reference the original equipment manufacturer service information.</li><li>Drain the lubricating oil. Refer to Procedure 007-037 in Section 7.</li><li>Remove the lubricating oil pan. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-007-025-tr\">Refer to Procedure 007-025 in Section 7.</a></li><li>Remove the lubricating oil filters. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-007-013-tr\">Refer to Procedure 007-013 in Section 7.</a></li><li>Remove the lubricating oil pan adapter assembly. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-007-027-tr\">Refer to Procedure 007-027 in Section 7.</a></li><li>Remove the oil suction tube and brackets. Refer to Procedure 007-035 in Section 7.</li><li>Remove the lubricating oil pump. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-007-031-tr\">Refer to Procedure 007-031 in Section 7.</a></li></ul><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ck800wa.png\" alt=\"ck800wa.png\"></figure><h3>Remove</h3><p>Check to make sure all of the main bearing caps are identified correctly.</p><p>The cylinder block has a number that is used to keep the block and main bearing caps together. The number is stamped on the bottom (oil pan flange) of the block at the left bank rear of the engine.</p><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>“709N” is used as an example <b>only</b>.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>The K38 and QSK38 main bearing caps are shown in this illustration. The K50 and QSK50 main bearing caps are numbered 1 through 9.</p></div></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/mb6caga.png\" alt=\"mb6caga.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout warning\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>CAUTION · Осторожно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Personal injury and damage to the crankshaft can result if the crankshaft is dropped.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Access to some side capscrews involves the removal of other components.</p></div></div><p>There are two side bolts for each main bearing cap.</p><p>Remove the main bearings one at a time.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/mb6csha.png\" alt=\"mb6csha.png\"></figure><p>Each main bearing cap contains the same number that is stamped on the block. The cap&#x27;s location number is on the cap in two places.</p><p>If the cap is <b>not</b> marked for location, it is strongly recommended that it be marked before removal from the cylinder block to aid identification.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/mb6cagb.png\" alt=\"mb6cagb.png\"></figure><p>Remove the main bearing cap mounting capscrews.</p><p>Remove the cap., Use the main bearing cap puller, Part Number 4919357.</p><p>Align the main bearing cap puller with the main bearing cap.</p><p>Install the fixture to the left and right oil pan rails of the engine block.</p><p>Install the main bearing cap puller clamp plate onto the main bearing cap and tighten the clamp knob.</p><p>Turn the adjusting nut at the top of the fixture with a wrench to lift the main bearing cap.</p><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>If the flywheel housing is still in place, the rear main bearing cap will need to be tilted forward slightly for tool access. Use a pry bar or a large screwdriver to tilt the cap forward.</p></div></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/22800640.png\" alt=\"22800640.png\"></figure><p>Remove the bearing from the cap and mark the location in the tang for future identification or analysis.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/mb800ma.png\" alt=\"mb800ma.png\"></figure><p>To remove the upper main bearing shells, use the main bearing remover, Part Number 3823818.</p><p>Rotate the crankshaft so the tang (1) of the main bearing shell rolls out of the block first.</p><p>Use the barring device to slowly rotate the crankshaft. Continue rotating the crankshaft until the bearing shell is out of the engine block. If the flywheel housing has been removed, the crankshaft can be turned using the crankshaft adapter mounting capscrews.</p><p>Mark the upper main bearing location in the tang area of the bearing.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600626.png\" alt=\"01600626.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>If bearing replacement was initiated due to damage, check the oil pan for debris. If significant debris is present, remove and disassemble the lubricating oil pump and inspect the bushing for debris damage. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-007-031-tr\">Refer to Procedure 007-031 in Section 7.</a></p></div></div><p>Clean the crankshaft journal with a lint-free cloth.</p><p>Inspect the journal for damage.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600324.png\" alt=\"01600324.png\"></figure><h3>Clean and Inspect for Reuse</h3><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>When using solvents, acids or alkaline materials for cleaning, follow the manufacture&#x27;s recommendations for use. Wear goggles and protective clothing to avoid personal injury.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Wear appropriate eye and face protection when using compressed air. Flying debris and dirt can cause personal injury.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout warning\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>CAUTION · Осторожно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Do not use a scraper or a wire brush, this can damage the bearings.</p></div></div><p>Make sure the bearings are marked for location. The bearing <b>must</b> be installed in its original location if it is to be used again.</p><p>Use solvent to clean the bearings.</p><p>Dry with compressed air.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/mb8bega.png\" alt=\"mb8bega.png\"></figure><p>Inspect the main bearing caps, capscrews, and washers for the following damage:</p><ul><li>Pitting</li><li>Cracking</li><li>Thread damage.</li></ul><p>Replace any damaged part.</p><p>Inspect the head of each capscrew to count the number of punch marks. Any capscrews with five punch marks on the head <b>must</b> be replaced.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01a00023.png\" alt=\"01a00023.png\"></figure><p>Inspect the bearings for damage.</p><p>Replace any bearings that have the following damage:</p><ul><li>Nicks</li><li>Cracks</li><li>Burrs</li><li>Scratches (deep enough to be felt with a fingernail)</li><li>Scrapes</li><li>Gouges</li><li>Embedded particles</li><li>Fretting</li><li>Pitting</li><li>Flaking</li><li>Corrosion</li><li>Lock tang damage.</li></ul><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/cx2besa.png\" alt=\"cx2besa.png\"></figure><p>Normal bearing wear produces a smooth finish, which will wear into the copper lining. Exposed copper does <b>not always</b> indicate worn bearings. Reference the Parts Reuse Guidelines, Bulletin 3810303.</p><p>If large areas of copper lining are visible in the bearings before the engine has accumulated 3000 hours, inspect the engine for contamination from fine dirt particles and correct the problem.</p><p>For more detailed information of bearing damage, reference the Analysis and Prevention of Bearing Failures Manual, Bulletin 3810387.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/cx8beba.png\" alt=\"cx8beba.png\"></figure><p>Use a ball-end micrometer. Measure the bearing thickness at the wear location. The bearing <b>must</b> be replaced if it is <b>not</b> within specifications.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Main Bearing Thickness Standard or Oversize (OS)</th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td></td><td>mm</td><td></td><td>in</td></tr><tr><td>Standard</td><td>4.280</td><td>MIN</td><td>0.1685</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>4.336</td><td>MAX</td><td>0.1707</td></tr><tr><td>0.010 (OS)</td><td>4.407</td><td>MIN</td><td>0.1735</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>4.463</td><td>MAX</td><td>0.1757</td></tr><tr><td>0.020 (OS)</td><td>4.534</td><td>MIN</td><td>0.1785</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>4.590</td><td>MAX</td><td>0.1807</td></tr><tr><td>0.030 (OS)</td><td>4.661</td><td>MIN</td><td>0.1835</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>4.717</td><td>MAX</td><td>0.1857</td></tr><tr><td>0.040 (OS)</td><td>4.788</td><td>MIN</td><td>0.1885</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>4.844</td><td>MAX</td><td>0.1907</td></tr></tbody></table></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/mb8betb.png\" alt=\"mb8betb.png\"></figure><h3>Install</h3><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>The location numbers of the main bearings begin with Number 1 at the front of the cylinder block.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>K38 bearings are numbered 1 through 7.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>K50 bearings are numbered 1 through 9.</p></div></div><p>Main bearings are two widths. The narrow bearings fit locations Number 1 and Number 7 in the K38 block, and Number 1 and Number 9 in the K50 and QSK50 block. The wide bearings fit the remaining locations in both blocks.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/mb6bega.png\" alt=\"mb6bega.png\"></figure><p>The upper bearings contain an oil hole. The lower bearings do <b>not</b>. Both bearings are marked on the back to indicate the location and either standard (STD) or oversize (OS). The amount of OS is stamped on the back in inches.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/mb6begb.png\" alt=\"mb6begb.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout warning\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>CAUTION · Осторожно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Prevent dirt from mixing with the lubricant. Dirty lubricant can cause low mileage failures.</p></div></div><p>Use a lint-free cloth. Clean the bearing and the mounting surface.</p><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Do <b>not</b> lubricate the back of the bearing.</p></div></div><p>Align the tang in the bearing with the slot in the block. Install the bearings. The end of the bearing <b>must</b> be even with the mounting surface for the main bearing cap.</p><p>Use engine oil if the engine will be operated immediately. Use Lubriplate™ 105, or equivalent, if the engine will <b>not</b> be operated immediately. Lubricate the bearings.</p><p>Reused bearings <b>mus</b> t be installed in their original location.</p><p>Install the upper main bearings shells. Use the same method that was used for the removal of the shells. Locate the rollout tool on the tang end of the bearing.</p><p>The bearing tang <b>must</b> fit into the slot in the bearing saddle to secure the bearing in the proper location.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600156.png\" alt=\"01600156.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout warning\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>CAUTION · Осторожно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Only newer K38 and K50 engines have the dowel ring (1) that protrudes from the main bearing cap. Do not install a cap with a dowel ring in the block that does not have a ring dowel on the other caps. If you are not sure, try to fit the dowel ring in the correct capscrew hole in the block. If it requires force, do not install the ring dowel.</p></div></div><p>If required, install the dowel ring.</p><p>Use a lint-free cloth to clean the lower bearings and the mounting surfaces of the cap.</p><p>Align the tang in the bearing with the slot in the main bearing cap. The end of the bearing <b>must</b> be even with the mounting surface of the main bearing cap.</p><p>Use the same lubricant that was applied on the upper main bearings. Lubricate the lower bearings.</p><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Do <b>not</b> lubricate the backs of the main bearings.</p></div></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/mb6caaa.png\" alt=\"mb6caaa.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout warning\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>CAUTION · Осторожно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Allow the excess oil to drip from the capscrews before installation in the block. Excess oil can cause hydraulic pressure, causing damage to the cylinder block.</p></div></div><p>Use clean engine oil to lubricate the capscrew heads, threads, and washers.</p><p>Install the washers and capscrews in the main bearing cap.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/mb6caaa.png\" alt=\"mb6caaa.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout warning\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>CAUTION · Осторожно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Do not rotate the crankshaft until the caps are pulled to the block. Damage can result if the bearings move out-of-location.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Make sure the side of the cap and bearing with the bearing locating tang is toward the tang in the block.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Keep the cap straight while moving it across the holes for the side bolts. There is little or no clearance between the parts.</p></div></div><p>Install the main bearing cap.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/mb6caha.png\" alt=\"mb6caha.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Install the capscrews by hand.</p></div></div><p>Turn the capscrews until they touch the washer or the main bearing cap.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/mb6cahb.png\" alt=\"mb6cahb.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Do <b>not</b> use impact wrenches. The main bearing shells can fall out.</p></div></div><p>Use both of the capscrews to pull the main bearing cap into position.</p><p>Use two wrenches. Tighten both of the capscrews at the same time.</p><p>Make sure the cap is touching the block. If it is <b>not</b>, check for a bearing out of location.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/mb6cahc.png\" alt=\"mb6cahc.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>The side of the lower thrust bearing support that is machined <b>must</b> be toward the main bearing cap.</p></div></div><p>Install the lower crankshaft thrust bearing support, lock washers, and capscrews. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-001-007-tr\">Refer to Procedure 001-007 in Section 1.</a> Refer to Procedure 001-007 in Section 1.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/mb6hshb.png\" alt=\"mb6hshb.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout warning\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>CAUTION · Осторожно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Do not rotate the crankshaft until the main bearing caps are pulled to the block. Damage will result if the bearings move out-of-location.</p></div></div><p>Turn the crankshaft with the barring tool. If it does <b>not</b> turn, remove the main bearing caps one at a time until the crankshaft turns freely. This helps to locate the cap or bearing that is <b>not</b> in alignment.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ks6shda.png\" alt=\"ks6shda.png\"></figure><p>Use clean engine oil to lubricate the capscrew heads, threads, and hardened plain washers.</p><p>Install the washers and capscrews for the side of the main bearing cap. Each cap requires two washers and capscrews.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/mb6csha.png\" alt=\"mb6csha.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>The torque sequence reference is for the straight torque method <b>only</b>.</p></div></div><p>For K38 and QSK38 engines, use the following steps to tighten the capscrews in the sequence shown.</p><p>Torque Value:</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/mb6booc.png\" alt=\"mb6booc.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>The torque sequence reference is for the straight torque method <b>only</b>.</p></div></div><p>For K50 and QSK50 engines, use the following steps to tighten the capscrews in the sequence shown.</p><p>Torque Value:</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/mb6booe.png\" alt=\"mb6booe.png\"></figure><p>Turn the crankshaft with your hands. If it will <b>not</b> turn, loosen the main bearing caps one at a time until the crankshaft turns freely. This helps to locate the cap that is <b>not</b> in alignment.</p><p>Check to make sure there is end clearance.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ks6shda.png\" alt=\"ks6shda.png\"></figure><p>Three different designs of capscrew have been used.</p><ol><li>12-point capscrews that are black</li><li>6-point capscrews that are light gray</li><li>6-point capscrews with angle marking (Part Number <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3628452\">3628452</a>).</li></ol><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Capscrews (1 and 2) use the torque procedure for tightening. They can be intermixed and installed in the same cap. Capscrew (3) uses the torque turn procedure for tightening.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>All engines built using the torque turn procedure are to have the torque turn procedure applied when rebuilt or repaired in the field. These engines can be identified by the capscrew markings. Older engines, unless block line bored, retain the old torque values. In cases where the torque turn procedure is <b>not</b> possible, due to limited access, a torque value of 814 N•m [600 ft-lb] can be used in place of the torque turn procedure.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>As the torque required for the torque turn procedure is significantly higher than the torque only procedure, Cummins Inc. recommends that a torque multiplier and breaker bar be used when advancing the capscrew the final 90 degrees. A standard torque wrench with 407 N•m [300 ft-lb] minimum capability, is to be used (without the torque multiplier) to obtain the initial 339 N•m [250 ft-lb] torque value.</p></div></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/mb6csxa.png\" alt=\"mb6csxa.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>The below torque sequence is for the torque plus angle method.</p></div></div><p>For K38 and QSK38 engines, tighten the main bearing cap mounting capscrews alternately and evenly, use the torque sequence shown.</p><p>Torque Value:</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/mb6booc.png\" alt=\"mb6booc.png\"></figure><p>For K50 and QSK50 engines, tighten the main bearing cap mounting capscrews alternately and evenly. Use the torque sequence shown.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/mb6booe.png\" alt=\"mb6booe.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>The below torque sequence is for the torque plus angle method.</p></div></div><p>Use a pencil or scribe and mark three marks on the main bearing caps adjacent to three of the marks on the head of the capscrew as shown. Use Dykem™ on the cap surface, if necessary, to make the marks visible.</p><p>Torque Value:</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/mb6csva.png\" alt=\"mb6csva.png\"></figure><p>Install the socket on the capscrew and scribe a line on the socket, in line with the single line on the cylinder block. Use Dykem™ on the socket for clarity. This socket is to fit snugly over the capscrew head. Use of a worn socket will reduce accuracy.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600296.png\" alt=\"01600296.png\"></figure><p>An alternative method for marking the cylinder block is to scribe the two lines 90 degrees apart on a socket. A template is to be used for accuracy. Place the socket over the capscrew and transfer one line to the cylinder block. Advance the capscrew 90 degrees. The second line on the socket needs to line up with the line on the block after the torque turn procedure is completed.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/mb6tona.png\" alt=\"mb6tona.png\"></figure><p>Place the socket over the capscrew with the mark in line with the single mark on the bearing cap.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600297.png\" alt=\"01600297.png\"></figure><p>Install a 7/16 inch capscrew, approximately 6 inches long, in the appropriate oil pan mounting capscrew hole to prevent the torque multiplier from turning when torque is applied to the main bearing capscrew.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/mb6csnf.png\" alt=\"mb6csnf.png\"></figure><p>Use the torque multiplier, as shown, to tighten the capscrew until the line on the socket is midway between the two marks on the main bearing cap.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/mb6csng.png\" alt=\"mb6csng.png\"></figure><p>Shown is the proper final location for the capscrew head markings relative to the cap markings.</p><p>Repeat the procedure for the remaining capscrew. Use the correct torque sequence.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/mb6csne.png\" alt=\"mb6csne.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Shown in this illustration is the K38 and QSK38. The K50 and QSK50 uses the same sequence, but is numbered 1 through 18.</p></div></div><p>Use the following steps to manually tighten the side bolts in the sequence shown.</p><p>Torque Value:</p><div class=\"fig-missing\">Иллюстрация <code>mb6bood.png</code> не выгружена — <a href=\"https://quickserve.cummins.com/rtgraphics/english/service/mb/6/mb6bood.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">открыть на QuickServe ↗</a></div><p>Check the crankshaft end clearance. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-001-007-tr\">Refer to Procedure 001-007 in Section 1.</a></p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ks6shja.png\" alt=\"ks6shja.png\"></figure><h3>Finishing Steps</h3><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>This component or assembly weighs greater than 23 kg [50 lb]. To prevent serious personal injury, be sure to have assistance or use appropriate lifting equipment to lift this component or assembly.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Batteries can emit explosive gases. To reduce the possibility of personal injury, always ventilate the compartment before servicing the batteries. To reduce the possibility of arcing, remove the negative (-) battery cable first and attach the negative (-) battery cable last.</p></div></div><ul><li>Install the lubricating oil pump. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-007-031-tr\">Refer to Procedure 007-031 in Section 7.</a></li><li>Install the oil suction tube and brackets. Refer to Procedure 007-035 in Section 7.</li><li>Install the lubricating oil pan. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-007-025-tr\">Refer to Procedure 007-025 in Section 7.</a></li><li>Install the lubricating oil pan adapter assembly. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-007-027-tr\">Refer to Procedure 007-027 in Section 7.</a></li><li>Install new lubricating oil filters. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-007-013-tr\">Refer to Procedure 007-013 in Section 7.</a></li><li>Fill the engine with lubricating oil. Refer to Procedure 007-037 in Section 7.</li><li>Connect the batteries. Reference the original equipment manufacturer service information.</li><li>Operate the engine and check for leaks.</li></ul><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ck800wa.png\" alt=\"ck800wa.png\"></figure>","28-001-007-tr":"<h3>Preparatory Steps</h3><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>This component or assembly weighs greater than 23 kg [50 lb]. To prevent serious personal injury, be sure to have assistance or use appropriate lifting equipment to lift this component or assembly.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Batteries can emit explosive gases. To reduce the possibility of personal injury, always ventilate the compartment before servicing the batteries. To reduce the possibility of arcing, remove the negative (-) battery cable first and attach the negative (-) battery cable last.</p></div></div><ul><li>Disconnect the batteries. Refer to the OEM service manual.</li><li>Drain the engine oil. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-007-025-tr\">Refer to Procedure 007-025 in Section.</a></li><li>Remove the oil filters. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-007-013-tr\">Refer to Procedure 007-013 in Section 7.</a></li><li>Remove the oil pan adapter assembly from the block flange. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-007-027-tr\">Refer to Procedure 007-027 in Section 7.</a></li><li>Remove all oil transfer and suction tubes and brackets. Refer to Procedure 007-035 in Section 7.</li><li>Remove all oil transfer and suction tubes and brackets. Refer to Procedure 001-061 in Section 1.</li></ul><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ck800wa.png\" alt=\"ck800wa.png\"></figure><h3>Remove</h3><p>Remove the two capscrews from the lower thrust bearing retainer and remove the retainer.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/mb6hshb.png\" alt=\"mb6hshb.png\"></figure><p>To remove the thrust bearing halves, keep the crankshaft at one end of its thrust and rotate it using the barring mechanism. If <b>only</b> one of the bearing halves rotates out, move the crankshaft to the other end of its thrust and rotate the crankshaft again.</p><p>Mark the location of each thrust bearing, front and rear, on the side opposite the one which faces the crankshaft flange.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600298.png\" alt=\"01600298.png\"></figure><p>Inspect the faces on the crankshaft thrust flange and the thrust surfaces of the bearing halves. Thrust bearings are available in standard and the following oversizes:</p><ul><li>0.254 mm [0.010 in]</li><li>0.508 mm [0.020 in]</li><li>0.762 mm [0.030 in]</li><li>1.016 mm [0.040 in]</li></ul><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600299.png\" alt=\"01600299.png\"></figure><h3>Inspect for Reuse</h3><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>When using solvents, acids, or alkaline materials for cleaning, follow the manufacturer&#x27;s recommendations for use. Wear goggles and protective clothing to reduce the possibility of personal injury.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Wear appropriate eye and face protection when using compressed air. Flying debris and dirt can cause personal injury.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout warning\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>CAUTION · Осторожно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Do not use a scraper or a wire brush. This can cause damage to the bearings.</p></div></div><p>Use solvent to clean the bearings.</p><p>Dry with compressed air.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/mb8bega.png\" alt=\"mb8bega.png\"></figure><p>Use a micrometer to measure the thrust bearing thickness at the wear location. The shine indicates where the crankshaft has contacted the bearing.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Thrust Bearing Thickness Standard or Oversize</th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td></td><td>mm</td><td></td><td>in</td></tr><tr><td>Standard</td><td>4.826</td><td>MIN</td><td>0.1900</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>4.940</td><td>MAX</td><td>0.1945</td></tr><tr><td>Oversize 0.254 mm [0.010 in]</td><td>5.080</td><td>MIN</td><td>0.2000</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>5.194</td><td>MAX</td><td>0.2045</td></tr><tr><td>Oversize 0.508 mm [0.020 in]</td><td>5.334</td><td>MIN</td><td>0.2100</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>5.448</td><td>MAX</td><td>0.2145</td></tr><tr><td>Oversize 0.762 mm [0.030 in]</td><td>5.588</td><td>MIN</td><td>0.2200</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>5.702</td><td>MAX</td><td>0.2245</td></tr><tr><td>Oversize 1.016 mm [0.040 in]</td><td>5.842</td><td>MIN</td><td>0.2300</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>5.956</td><td>MAX</td><td>0.2345</td></tr></tbody></table></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/mb8betb.png\" alt=\"mb8betb.png\"></figure><h3>Install</h3><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>The grooves in the thrust bearings <b>must</b> face the flange on the crankshaft.</p></div></div><p>Lubricate the two thrust bearings with clean engine oil and insert them into the thrust bearing support.</p><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Move the crankshaft to the front or rear as necessary to insert the second thrust bearing.</p></div></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600301.png\" alt=\"01600301.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>The machined side of the lower thrust bearing retainer <b>must</b> be toward the main bearing cap.</p></div></div><p>Install the thrust bearing retainer, lock washer, and capscrews.</p><div class=\"callout tip\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">🔧</span>Момент затяжки · Torque Value</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>40 n•m [30 ft-lb]</p></div></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/mb6hshb.png\" alt=\"mb6hshb.png\"></figure><p>Use a dial indicator to measure end clearance. Move the crankshaft to one end of its thrust and zero the dial. Move the crankshaft to the other end of its thrust and take the reading.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Crankshaft End Clearance</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>mm</td><td></td><td>in</td></tr><tr><td>0.13</td><td>MIN</td><td>0.005</td></tr><tr><td>0.51</td><td>MAX</td><td>0.020</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>If the clearance is <b>not</b> within specifications, check for foreign material on the thrust bearings or crankshaft flange. Oversize thrust bearings are available to adjust the end clearance.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ks6shja.png\" alt=\"ks6shja.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>It is recommended to recheck the crankshaft end clearance once the engine is installed into an application.</p></div></div><p>Release the tension on the cooling fan drive belt, if installed. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-008-002-tr\">Refer to Procedure 008-002 in Section 8.</a></p><p>Remove an accessible hand hole cover. Refer to Procedure 001-035 in Section 1.</p><p>Use the barring device to rotate the engine so that the connecting rod journal is opposite the hand hole cover opening. If the connecting rods are already in this position when the hand hole cover is removed, use the barring device to rotate the crankshaft slightly in the opposite direction to normal rotation. This releases the loading on the gear train.</p><p>Attach a dial indicator, resting against the vibration damper.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ks400dc.png\" alt=\"ks400dc.png\"></figure><p>Use a pry bar or similar to move the crankshaft backwards by prying against the crankshaft web.</p><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Do <b>not</b> pry against the connecting rods or crankshaft counterweights.</p></div></div><p>Set the dial indicator to zero.</p><p>Move the crankshaft in the opposite direction by prying on the crankshaft web.</p><p>Check the reading on the dial indicator to confirm the crankshaft has end clearance, which is within the limits shown above.</p><p>If previously loosened, tension the cooling fan drive belt. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-008-002-tr\">Refer to Procedure 008-002 in Section 8.</a></p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01s00036.png\" alt=\"01s00036.png\"></figure><h3>Finishing Steps</h3><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>This component weighs greater than 23 kg [50 lb]. To prevent serious personal injury, be sure to have assistance or use appropriate lifting equipment to lift this component or assembly.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Batteries can emit explosive gases. To reduce the possibility of personal injury, always ventilate the compartment before servicing the batteries. To reduce the possibility of arcing, remove the negative (-) battery cable first and attach the negative (-) battery cable last.</p></div></div><ul><li>Install oil transfer and suction tubes and brackets. Refer to Procedure 007-035 in Section 7.</li><li>Install oil transfer and suction tubes and brackets. Refer to Procedure 001-061 in Section 1.</li><li>Install the oil pan adapter assembly to the block flange using a new gasket. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-007-027-tr\">Refer to Procedure 007-027 in Section 7.</a></li><li>Install new oil filters. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-007-013-tr\">Refer to Procedure 007-013 in Section 7.</a></li><li>Fill the engine with clean 15W-40 engine oil. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-007-025-tr\">Refer to Procedure 007-025 in Section 7.</a></li><li>Connect the batteries. Refer to the OEM service manual.</li><li>Operate the engine and check for leaks.</li></ul><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ck800wa.png\" alt=\"ck800wa.png\"></figure>","28-001-010-tr":"<h3>Remove</h3><p>The K38 and K50 engines contain two camshaft bushings in each bore, except in the bores in the left bank (LB) front and right bank (RB) rear. The LB front and RB rear contain one busing <b>only</b>.</p><p>When the engines are manufactured, a bushing, Part Number <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3175805\">3175805</a>, which does <b>not</b> contain an internal oil groove, is installed in all bores except the LB front and RB rear. To reduce confusion in the field, <b>only</b> the bushing, Part Number 205230, with an internal oil groove, is available for service.</p><p>If a bushing in a bore that contains two bushings <b>must</b> be replaced, both bushings <b>must</b> be replaced. It is recommended that if one bushing of the engine bank <b>must</b> be replaced, that all of the bushings for that bank be replaced.</p><p>Use the camshaft bushing driver assembly, Part Number 3376637, and the driver kit, Part Number 3376634.</p><p>For ease of removal/installation, hydraulic cam bushing tools are available. They include hydraulic actuator kit, Part Number 3823621, installation/removal kit, Part Number 3823647, and centering guide kit, Part Number 3823653.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ka3bswa.png\" alt=\"ka3bswa.png\"></figure><p>Use the driver assembly (1). Remove the camshaft bushing (2) from the bore that contains one bushing.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/cg6bsma.png\" alt=\"cg6bsma.png\"></figure><p>Use the driver assembly (1). Push one of the bushings (2) until it touches the other. Push both bushings (2) out at the same time.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/cg6bsmb.png\" alt=\"cg6bsmb.png\"></figure><h3>Clean and Inspect for Reuse</h3><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>When using solvents, acids, or alkaline materials for cleaning, follow the manufacturer&#x27;s recommendations for use. Wear goggles and protective clothing to reduce the possibility of personal injury.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Wear appropriate eye and face protection when using compressed air. Flying debris and dirt can cause bodily injury.</p></div></div><p>Use an emery cloth. Remove any burrs and sharp edges from the bore.</p><p>Use solvent to clean the bore.</p><p>Dry with compressed air.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/cg6brta.png\" alt=\"cg6brta.png\"></figure><p>Measure the inside diameter.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Camshaft Bushing Bore in the Block</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>mm</td><td></td><td>in</td></tr><tr><td>80.949</td><td>MIN</td><td>3.1869</td></tr><tr><td>81.013</td><td>MAX</td><td>3.1894</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>If the inside diameter is <b>not</b> within specifications, repair the block by installing a repair sleeve.</p><p>See the Alternative Repair Manual, Bulletin 3379035.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/cg6brta.png\" alt=\"cg6brta.png\"></figure><h3>Install</h3><p>To install the first bushing in the bores that require two bushings:</p><ul><li>Position the bushing (2) so that the clinch joint of the bushing (3) is pointed toward the cylinder head surface of the block. The elongated hole (4) is to be on the side of the bore that is nearest to the vee of the block.</li><li>Use a driver assembly. Push the bushing through the bore. The bushing <b>must</b> be even with, or no more than 1.6 mm [0.0625 inch] below the surface of the block web (5).</li></ul><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/cg6bsha.png\" alt=\"cg6bsha.png\"></figure><p>Position the second bushing (2) for the bore as described previously.</p><ul><li>Use the driver assembly. Push the bushing in the bore until it is even with, or no more than 1.6 mm [0.0625 inch] below the surface of the block web (5).</li><li>Make sure the oil hole (6) is open and clean.</li></ul><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/cg6bshb.png\" alt=\"cg6bshb.png\"></figure><p>To install the single bushings at the LB front and RB rear:</p><ul><li>Position the bushing (2) so that the elongated hole in the bushing (4) is in line with the oil hole in the block (6). The oil holes in the bushing will be nearest to the cylinder block. The clinch joint (3) will be pointed toward the outboard side of the bore.</li><li>Use the driver assembly. Push the bushing in the bore until it is even with, or no more than 1.6 mm [0.0625 inch] below the surface of the block web (5).</li><li>Check to make sure the oil hole in the block (6) is aligned with the elongated hole in the bushing (4).</li><li>Make sure the oil hole (6) is open and clean.</li></ul><div class=\"fig-missing\">Иллюстрация <code>cg6bshc.png</code> не выгружена — <a href=\"https://quickserve.cummins.com/rtgraphics/english/service/cg/6/cg6bshc.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">открыть на QuickServe ↗</a></div>","28-001-016-tr":"<h3>Preparatory Steps</h3><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Batteries can emit explosive gases. To reduce the possibility of personal injury, always ventilate the compartment before servicing the batteries. To reduce the possibility of arcing, remove the negative (-) battery cable first and attach the negative (-) battery cable last.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Some state and federal agencies have determined that used engine oil can be carcinogenic and cause reproductive toxicity. Avoid inhalation of vapors, ingestion, and prolonged contact with used engine oil. If not reused, dispose of in accordance with local environmental regulations.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>To reduce the possibility of personal injury, avoid direct contact of hot oil with your skin.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>This component or assembly weighs greater than 23 kg [50 lb] or more. To prevent serious personal injury, be sure to have assistance or use appropriate lifting equipment lift this component or assembly.</p></div></div><ul><li>Disconnect the batteries. See equipment manufacturer service information.</li><li>Disconnect the air/fuel supply to the air starter, if equipped. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/102-012-022\">Refer to Procedure 012-022 in Section 12.</a></li><li>Drain the oil. Refer to Procedure 007-037 in Section 7.</li><li>Remove the full flow filters. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-007-013-tr\">Refer to Procedure 007-013 in Section 7.</a></li><li>Remove the oil suction tube. Refer to Procedure 007-035 in Section 7.</li><li>Remove the oil pan adapter. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-007-027-tr\">Refer to Procedure 007-027 in Section 7.</a></li><li>Remove the engine and place on an engine stand. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-000-001-tr\">Refer to Procedure 000-001 in Section 0.</a></li><li>Remove the flexplate, if installed. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-016-004-tr\">Refer to Procedure 016-004 in Section 16.</a></li><li>Remove the flywheel, if installed. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-016-005-tr\">Refer to Procedure 016-005 in Section 16.</a></li><li>Remove the flywheel housing. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-016-006-tr\">Refer to Procedure 016-006 in Section 16.</a></li><li>Remove the front gear cover. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-001-031-tr\">Refer to Procedure 001-031 in Section 1.</a></li><li>Remove the connecting rod bearing caps. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-001-054-tr\">Refer to Procedure 001-054 in Section 1.</a></li><li>Remove the main bearing caps. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-001-006-tr\">Refer to Procedure 001-006 in Section 1.</a></li><li>Remove the cylinder head. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-002-004-tr\">Refer to Procedure 002-004 in Section 2</a>.</li><li>Remove the piston and connecting rod assembly. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-001-054-tr\">Refer to Procedure 001-054 in Section 1.</a></li></ul><h3>Remove</h3><p>Remove the two capscrews. Remove the lower thrust bearing support.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/mb6hshb.png\" alt=\"mb6hshb.png\"></figure><p>Remove and mark both crankshaft thrust bearings.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/mb6behb.png\" alt=\"mb6behb.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>This component or assembly weighs greater than 23 kg [50 lb] or more. To prevent serious personal injury, be sure to have assistance or use appropriate lifting equipment lift this component or assembly.</p></div></div><p>Use a nylon sling and a hoist to remove the crankshaft.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ks6shha.png\" alt=\"ks6shha.png\"></figure><p>Remove and mark the upper main bearings. Mark the location of the bearing on the tang.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/mb6beha.png\" alt=\"mb6beha.png\"></figure><p>Remove the six capscrews.</p><p>Remove the crankshaft thrust bearing support.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/mb6hsha.png\" alt=\"mb6hsha.png\"></figure><h3>Clean and Inspect for Reuse</h3><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>When using solvents, acids or alkaline materials for cleaning, follow the manufacturer&#x27;s recommendations for use. Wear goggles and protective clothing to reduce the possibility of personal injury.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Wear appropriate eye and face protection when using compressed air. Flying debris and dirt can cause personal injury.</p></div></div><p>Clean the crankshaft using solvent and dry with compressed air.</p><p>Inspect the crankshaft for nicks or scratches. If a mark can <b>not</b> be felt with a fingernail, there is no value in polishing the crankshaft.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ks8bdec.png\" alt=\"ks8bdec.png\"></figure><p>Use a Scotch-Brite™ 7448 abrasive pad, Part Number 3823258, or equivalent, to remove discoloration or light scratches from the machined surfaces.</p><p>The crankshaft <b>must</b> be cleaned again if this polishing is performed.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ks8bded.png\" alt=\"ks8bded.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>When using solvents, acids or alkaline materials for cleaning, follow the manufacturer&#x27;s recommendations for use. Wear goggles and protective clothing to reduce the possibility of personal injury.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Wear appropriate eye and face protection when using compressed air. Flying debris and dirt can cause personal injury.</p></div></div><p>The K2000E, K1800E, and K1500E engines use a crankshaft having a 77.47 mm [3.050 in] diameter bolt circle in the nose. The threads are rolled, <b>not</b> cut, as on the earlier K50 crankshafts.</p><p>To clean the rolled threads, flush with solvent. Dry the threads with compressed air. If additional cleaning is required, brush with a nylon bristle brush.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ks600sa.png\" alt=\"ks600sa.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>When using solvents, acids or alkaline materials for cleaning, follow the manufacturer&#x27;s recommendations for use. Wear goggles and protective clothing to reduce the possibility of personal injury.</p></div></div><p>Use solvent and a bristle brush to clean all oil drillings.</p><p>Use a light preservative oil to lubricate the part to prevent rust.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ks6sheb.png\" alt=\"ks6sheb.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>When using solvents, acids or alkaline materials for cleaning, follow the manufacturer&#x27;s recommendations for use. Wear goggles and protective clothing to reduce the possibility of personal injury.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>New crankshafts are coated with a heavy preservative. Use solvent to thoroughly remove the coating. Brush or flush the packing debris from the oil drillings before installing the crankshaft in the engine.</p></div></div><p>Use a hone stone to polish the outside diameter surface at the front and rear oil seal locations, the flywheel mounting location, and the vibration damper location. Remove all small scratches and grooves.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ks6shea.png\" alt=\"ks6shea.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>When using solvents, acids or alkaline materials for cleaning, follow the manufacturer&#x27;s recommendations for use. Wear goggles and protective clothing to reduce the possibility of personal injury.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Wear appropriate eye and face protection when using compressed air. Flying debris and dirt can cause personal injury.</p></div></div><p>Use solvent to clean the crankshaft.</p><p>Dry with compressed air.</p><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Make sure to blow out the threaded holes on each end of the crankshaft and the oil drillings. Use a light preservative oil to prevent rust during engine rebuild. Tape over the thread holes.</p></div></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ks8bdec.png\" alt=\"ks8bdec.png\"></figure><p>The webs of the crankshaft are numbered from the front of the crankshaft. The number is steel-stamped on the web.</p><p>The K50 crankshaft contains 16 webs (not shown).</p><p>The K38 crankshaft contains 12 webs, as illustrated.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ks600gb.png\" alt=\"ks600gb.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout warning\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>CAUTION · Осторожно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>If the counterweights are removed, they must be installed in the correct position on the same crankshaft. If they are installed in the wrong position, engine damage can occur.</p></div></div><p>K50 crankshafts contain counterweights that are bolted. The counterweights have the same number as the web to which they are attached. The K50 crankshaft contains eight counterweights.</p><p>If a counterweight <b>must</b> be replaced, the crankshaft <b>must</b> be balanced again. See the Alternative Repair Manual, Bulletin 3379035.</p><p>Newer crankshafts contain a ring dowel for the counterweights. The counterweights are <b>not</b> interchangeable between the newer and older crankshafts.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ks600gc.png\" alt=\"ks600gc.png\"></figure><p>Measure the outside diameter at the locations shown.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Crankshaft Outside Diameter</th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td></td><td>mm</td><td></td><td>in</td></tr><tr><td>Location (1)</td><td>110.74</td><td>MIN</td><td>4.360</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>110.77</td><td>MAX</td><td>4.361</td></tr><tr><td>Location (2)</td><td>111.07</td><td>MIN</td><td>4.373</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>111.13</td><td>MAX</td><td>4.375</td></tr><tr><td>Location (3)</td><td>184.10</td><td>MIN</td><td>7.248</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>184.15</td><td>MAX</td><td>7.250</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Check the threads for damage at both ends of the crankshaft.</p><p>If necessary, see the Alternative Repair Manual, Bulletin 3379035, for repair instructions.</p><p>Check the roll pins (4) for damage. Replace the pins if they are damaged.</p><p>The roll pins <b>must</b> be installed because they align the vibration damper and the flywheel with the crankshaft. The vibration damper and flywheel contain the index marks required for proper adjustment of the valves and injectors.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ks6shta.png\" alt=\"ks6shta.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout warning\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>CAUTION · Осторожно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>K2000E, K1800E, and K1500E engines use ROLLED threads in the crankshaft nose. Do not use a thread cutting tap to chase or clean the threads, or damage or failure can result.</p></div></div><p>Thread salvage inserts are <b>not</b> permitted in the crankshaft nose.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ks600va.png\" alt=\"ks600va.png\"></figure><p>Inspect the crankshaft seal contact areas for wear. The wear groove is acceptable if it is continuous and no more than 0.12 mm [0.05 in] deep.</p><p>If the wear groove depth is greater than the specification above, the crankshaft <b>must</b> be replaced.</p><p>If the wear groove depth is less than the specification above, a wear sleeve can be installed to repair the surface. Use the following procedure for front crankshaft seal installation with a wear sleeve. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-001-023-tr\">Refer to Procedure 001-023 in Section 1.</a></p><p>Installation of a repair sleeve to the front crankshaft nose is recommended during rebuild to protect the crankshaft nose.</p><p>A repair sleeve is <b>not</b> available for the rear crankshaft nose as it is already incorporated into the rear crankshaft seal.</p><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>The crankshaft nose must not be repaired using any metal spray or welding process.</p></div></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01500035.png\" alt=\"01500035.png\"></figure><p>Check the main bearing journals and the rod bearing journals for damage or excessive wear. Minor scratches are acceptable.</p><p>If a fingernail catches in the scratch, the crankshaft <b>must</b> be replaced or machined. See the Alternative Repair Manual, Bulletin 3379035.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ks6shsa.png\" alt=\"ks6shsa.png\"></figure><p>Measure the outside diameter of all the journals and the thrust flange thickness.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Crankshaft Main Bearing Journal Outside Diameter (6)</th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td></td><td>mm</td><td></td><td>in</td></tr><tr><td>Standard</td><td>165.05</td><td>MIN</td><td>6.498</td></tr><tr><td>165.10</td><td>MAX</td><td>6.500</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Undersize 0.25 mm [0.010 in]</td><td>164.80</td><td>MIN</td><td>6.488</td></tr><tr><td>164.85</td><td>MAX</td><td>6.490</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Undersize 0.50 mm [0.020 in]</td><td>164.55</td><td>MIN</td><td>6.478</td></tr><tr><td>164.60</td><td>MAX</td><td>6.480</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Undersize 0.76 mm [0.030 in]</td><td>164.29</td><td>MIN</td><td>6.468</td></tr><tr><td>164.34</td><td>MAX</td><td>6.470</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Undersize 1.016 mm [0.040 in]</td><td>164.03</td><td>MIN</td><td>6.458</td></tr><tr><td>164.08</td><td>MAX</td><td>6.460</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Crankshaft Connecting Rod Journal Outside Diameter (5)</th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td></td><td>mm</td><td></td><td>in</td></tr><tr><td>Standard</td><td>107.87</td><td>MIN</td><td>4.247</td></tr><tr><td>107.95</td><td>MAX</td><td>4.250</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Undersize 0.25 mm [0.010 in]</td><td>107.62</td><td>MIN</td><td>4.237</td></tr><tr><td>107.70</td><td>MAX</td><td>4.240</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Undersize 0.50 mm [0.020 in]</td><td>107.37</td><td>MIN</td><td>4.227</td></tr><tr><td>107.45</td><td>MAX</td><td>4.230</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Undersize 0.75 mm [0.030 in]</td><td>107.11</td><td>MIN</td><td>4.217</td></tr><tr><td>107.19</td><td>MAX</td><td>4.220</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Undersize 1.016 mm [0.040 in]</td><td>106.85</td><td>MIN</td><td>4.207</td></tr><tr><td>106.93</td><td>MAX</td><td>4.210</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>The crankshaft can be machined undersize if the outside diameter is <b>not</b> within specifications. <b>Always</b> grind all the journals when one is <b>not</b> within specifications.</p></div></div><p>Oversize rod bearings and main bearings are available. See the Alternative Repair Manual, Bulletin 3379035, for grinding specifications and instructions.</p><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Oversize thrust bearings are available if the thrust distance is <b>not</b> within specifications. Bearings that are 0.25 mm [0.010 in], 0.51 mm [0.020 in], 0.76 mm [0.030 in], and 1.02 mm [0.040 in] thicker than standard are available. If the crankshaft <b>must</b> be machined for an oversize thrust bearing, see the Alternative Repair Manual, Bulletin 3379035.</p></div></div><p>Use a light preservative oil to lubricate the crankshaft to prevent rust. If the crankshaft is <b>not</b> going to be installed immediately, use a heavy preservative oil.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ks6shtb.png\" alt=\"ks6shtb.png\"></figure><p>Measure the thrust flange.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Thrust Flange (7)</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>mm</td><td></td><td>in</td></tr><tr><td>12.52</td><td>MIN</td><td>0.493</td></tr><tr><td>12.75</td><td>MAX</td><td>0.502</td></tr></tbody></table></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ks6shtb.png\" alt=\"ks6shtb.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout warning\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>CAUTION · Осторожно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>The capscrews must be tight or the counterweight can fall, causing severe damage to the engine. Any loose capscrews must be replaced with new capscrews.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout warning\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>CAUTION · Осторожно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Capscrews cannot be used again and must be replaced with new capscrews.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout warning\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>CAUTION · Осторожно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Check new capscrews for any visible damage or wear. Discard the capscrew if there is any visible damage or wear to the thread, neck, or capscrew head.</p></div></div><p>Use a torque wrench to check the torque of the counterweight capscrews. Each counterweight contains two capscrews.</p><div class=\"callout tip\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">🔧</span>Момент затяжки · Torque Value</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>285 n•m [210 ft-lb]</p></div></div><p>Mark any counterweights that have a loose capscrew.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ks6bosa.png\" alt=\"ks6bosa.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>The following step applies <b>only</b> to the counterweights that contain a loose capscrew.</p></div></div><p>Remove the capscrews (8) and washers (9).</p><p>Remove the counterweight.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ks6boma.png\" alt=\"ks6boma.png\"></figure><p>Check the counterweight and crankshaft for fretting or damage between the mating surfaces. If the counterweight is damaged, the counterweight <b>must</b> be replaced and the crankshaft <b>must</b> be balanced. If the crankshaft is damaged, the crankshaft <b>must</b> be replaced or the counterweight replaced and the crankshaft repaired.</p><p><b>Not</b> more than 0.51 mm [0.020 in] can be removed from the crankshaft mounting pad to repair fretting.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600656.png\" alt=\"01600656.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout warning\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>CAUTION · Осторожно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>If the counterweight is removed, it must be installed with the part number toward the nearest main bearing journal. If the counterweight is not installed correctly, the weight can strike the connecting rod, causing extensive damage to the engine.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Make sure the counterweight is returned to its original location. The counterweights have the same number stamped on them as the web to which they are attached.</p></div></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600658.png\" alt=\"01600658.png\"></figure><p>There <b>must not</b> be an interference between the crankshaft and counterweight and the sides of the mounting pad.</p><p>Lubricate the washers and capscrews with clean engine oil.</p><p>Install the washers (9) and capscrews (8).</p><p>Tighten the capscrews.</p><div class=\"callout tip\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">🔧</span>Момент затяжки · Torque Value</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>285 n•m [210 ft-lb]</p></div></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600655.png\" alt=\"01600655.png\"></figure><p>With the capscrews tightened, visually inspect the counterweight for a interference condition with the crank.</p><p>There <b>must not</b> be an interference between the crankshaft and counterweight and the sides of the mounting pad.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600663.png\" alt=\"01600663.png\"></figure><p>Inspect the mating surface of the thrust bearing support for damage.</p><p>Use emery or crocus cloth, if necessary, to remove any burrs or other imperfections.</p><p>Clean the thrust bearing support and retainer thoroughly.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600633.png\" alt=\"01600633.png\"></figure><h3>Magnetic Crack Inspect</h3><p>Use a magnetic particle testing machine.</p><p>Perform the head shot and inspection method. Then perform the coil shot and inspection method.</p><p>Adjust the machine to 1800 ampere VDC or rectified VAC.</p><p>Use the continuous method. Wet <b>only</b> 1/3 of the crankshaft at a time.</p><p>Check the crankshaft for cracks.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ks8bdsa.png\" alt=\"ks8bdsa.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Ampere turn is an electrical current of one ampere flowing through the coil, multiplied by the number of turns in the coil.</p></div></div><p>Use the coil shot method. Use a coil that is a minimum of 514 mm [20.250 in] in diameter.</p><p>Use the continuous method to apply coil shot.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Amperage - (Ampere Turns)</th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Minimum</td><td>4500 VDC or Rectified VAC</td></tr><tr><td>Maximum</td><td>5000 VDC or Rectified VAC</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Check the crankshaft for cracks.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ks8bdsb.png\" alt=\"ks8bdsb.png\"></figure><p>Limits of Acceptance - Open Indications:</p><p>An open indication is visible to the eye after the wetting operation has been completed. An indication below the surface is <b>not</b> visible to the eye after the wetting operation has been completed. An indication below the surface can be seen with the use of the ultraviolet light that is part of the machine.</p><p>Do <b>not</b> use the crankshaft if:</p><ul><li>There is an indication in the fillet or in the shaded area</li><li>There is an indication that passes through the 45 degree diagonal from the oil hole or goes into the oil hole chamfer</li><li>There is an indication that is longer than 6 mm [0.250 inch]</li><li>There are more than four indications on one journal.</li></ul><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ks8bdna.png\" alt=\"ks8bdna.png\"></figure><p>Limits of Acceptance - Indications Below the Surface:</p><p>Do <b>not</b> use the crankshaft if:</p><ul><li>There is an indication in the fillet or in the shaded area that is in a circumferential direction.</li><li>There is an indication in a circumferential direction that is longer than 25.4 mm [1.00 in].</li><li>There is an indication in an axial direction that is longer than 9.5 mm [0.375 in].</li><li>There is an indication that is closer than 1.5 mm [0.0625 in] to an oil hole chamfer.</li><li>There is an indication that passes through the 45 degree diagonal from the oil hole.</li></ul><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ks8bdnb.png\" alt=\"ks8bdnb.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Wear protective clothing to prevent personal injury from burns.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout warning\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>CAUTION · Осторожно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>The crankshaft must be demagnetized completely and cleaned thoroughly. Small metal particles can cause engine damage.</p></div></div><p>Demagnetize the crankshaft.</p><p>Use steam to clean the crankshaft and the oil drillings.</p><p>Use a light preservative oil to lubricate the crankshaft to prevent rust.</p><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Use a heavy preservative oil if the crankshaft is <b>not</b> going to be installed immediately. Protect the part with a cover to prevent dirt from sticking to the oil.</p></div></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ks600gd.png\" alt=\"ks600gd.png\"></figure><h3>Bend and Twist Inspect</h3><p>The crankshaft straightness is determined by the amount of total runout and the adjacent journal runout.</p><p>The following definitions will help in understanding the procedure:</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/10600725.png\" alt=\"10600725.png\"></figure><ul><li>Journal Runout: The top indicator reading (total sweep of the needle) of the main bearing journal as the crankshaft is rotated one complete revolution (360 degrees). Total runout refers to the maximum defection taken at the journal in the center.</li><li>Adjacent Journal Runout: The relationship of the top indicator reading of a main bearing journal as it is rotated on a common axis to the top indicator reading of an adjacent journal.</li></ul><p>Adjacent Journal Runout is often referred to as the step runout, the bearing-to-bearing runout, or the journal-to-journal runout.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ks8bdtb.png\" alt=\"ks8bdtb.png\"></figure><ul><li>Clock Position: The location of the journal at the highest top indicator reading point. Compare its angular relationship with the number 1 crankshaft pin as viewed from the front of the crankshaft. In this example, the crankshaft pin is at the 8 o&#x27;clock position. This is the clock position of the journal being measured.</li></ul><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ks8bdtc.png\" alt=\"ks8bdtc.png\"></figure><p>Measurement of crankshaft straightness with the specified accuracy can <b>only</b> be achieved if the crankshaft:</p><ul><li>Is correctly supported</li><li>Is at thermal equilibrium</li><li>Can be rotated smoothly.</li></ul><p>These requirements can <b>not</b> be met by measuring the crankshaft while supported by bearing shells in a cylinder block. Rotation of the crankshaft with such an arrangement will lead to errors as the result of lateral and vertical displacement arising from curvature and clearance in the main bearings.</p><h4>Measurement Environment</h4><ul><li>Measurement <b>must</b> be conducted in a temperature controlled room at a temperature of 20°C [ 68°F ] +/- 1 degree.</li><li>The crankshaft to be measured <b>must</b> be stored in this room for at least twenty four hours to allow the crankshaft to stabilize at room temperature.</li><li>Temperature of the crankshaft <b>must</b> be verified before measurements starts, and recorded on the measurement data worksheet included in this document. See measurement data worksheet section 1.8. below.</li></ul><h4>Measurement Setup</h4><ul><li>The measurement table <b>must</b> be rated to support at least 450 kg [ 992 lb ] and provide a stable, flat surface that allows V-blocks to be aligned and secured with the crankshaft centerline in a straight horizontal position.</li></ul><p>The use of a granite measurement table and roller V-blocks are recommended. See Figure 1. Roller V-blocks have lower rolling resistance than standard V-blocks when rotating the crankshaft during measurement process.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01k00101.png\" alt=\"01k00101.png\"></figure><p>Figure 1.</p><h4>Figure 1: Crankshaft on Roller V-blocks</h4><ul><li>A motorized drive is preferred to be used to rotate the crankshaft smoothly over the full 360 degrees of rotation. Direct manual rotation <b>must</b> be done very carefully to provide the smooth motion necessary for consistent measurements.</li><li>The height of V-blocks <b>must</b> be equal (Z-axis). The height difference between the V-blocks <b>must</b> be much smaller than part specified tolerance.</li></ul><p>Example: If straightness specification is less than 0.06 mm [ 0.002 in ] then the height difference <b>must</b> be 0.01 mm [ 0.0004 in ] or less. The V-blocks height <b>must</b> be verified in the contact points of V-blocks with the crankshaft journal using a calibrated shaft or bar. Measurements can be made with a mechanical dial indicator or an electronic precision measurement system.</p><ul><li>The measuring device used <b>must</b> have a valid calibration certificate and <b>must</b> have resolution, repeatability, and accuracy (relationship of the real dimension to what the measurement device indicates) appropriate for the straightness specification being evaluated.</li></ul><p>Place two V-blocks on a flat surface. Support the crankshaft on the V-blocks on main journal number 2 and main journal number 8, as shown.</p><p>Verify that the crankshaft is free <b>only</b> to rotate, with no vertical or lateral motion as the result of clearance in the rollers or rocking of the V-blocks.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01s00116.png\" alt=\"01s00116.png\"></figure><p>Use a dial indicator to position the indicator so the stem touches the bearing journal being measured at the journal centerline.</p><p>The point of measurement chosen <b>must</b> be used for measuring all bearing journals.</p><p>The K50 crankshaft has nine main bearing journals (not shown).</p><p>The K38 crankshaft has seven main bearing journals, as illustrated.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ks8bdte.png\" alt=\"ks8bdte.png\"></figure><p>Rotate the crankshaft and measure the top indicator reading at each bearing journal. Record the value and the clock position for each journal.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ks8bdtf.png\" alt=\"ks8bdtf.png\"></figure><p>Check the part number to determine if the crankshaft is a fully fillet hardened type, or is a non-fully fillet hardened type. Refer to Procedure 001-999 in Section F.</p><p>A fully fillet hardened or shot-peened crankshaft <b>must</b> be discarded if the total runout is <b>not</b> within specifications. A crankshaft that has no fillet treatment can be straightened. Refer to the Alternative Repair Manual, Bulletin 3379035.</p><p>Determine the Crankshaft Total Runout</p><p>Compare the runout to the specifications.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Crankshaft Total Runout</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td></td><td>mm</td><td></td><td>in</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>K38 Engine</td><td></td><td>0.203</td><td>MAX</td><td>0.0081</td></tr><tr><td>K50 Engine</td><td></td><td>0.267</td><td>MAX</td><td>0.0105</td></tr></tbody></table></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/10600725.png\" alt=\"10600725.png\"></figure><p>Determine the Adjacent Journal Runout:</p><p>The example illustrates the following plot points.</p><p>For each journal, plot the top indicator reading value at its clock position on a polar chart illustrated on the following page.</p><p>The end journals supported by V-blocks <b>must</b> be plotted at the center of the chart.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Journal</th><th>Top Indicator Reading</th><th>Clock Position</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.0021</td><td>12</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.0030</td><td>12</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>0.0039</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Draw a straight line between the plotted points, (from journal number 1 to number 2, and from number 2 to number 3), until all journals are plotted on the chart.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ks8bdtg.png\" alt=\"ks8bdtg.png\"></figure><p>To determine the adjacent journal runout, measure the length of the line from each journal to its corresponding journal point.</p><p>Illustrated as an example, journal number 3 to number 4 is 5.1 mm [2 in]. This represents a runout of 0.051 mm [0.002 in].</p><p>Record the adjacent journal runout for each bearing journal. Compare the adjacent journal runout to specifications.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Adjacent Journal Runout</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td></td><td>mm</td><td></td><td>in</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Fully Fillet Hardened</td><td></td><td>0.089</td><td>MAX</td><td>0.0035</td></tr><tr><td>Non-Fully Fillet Hardened</td><td></td><td>0.089</td><td>MAX</td><td>0.0035</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Replace the crankshaft if any measurement is <b>not</b> within specifications.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01400348.png\" alt=\"01400348.png\"></figure><p>Polar Chart</p><p>Scale 1 inch = 0.001 inch and 1 mm = 0.001 mm</p><p>The small graduations are 0.1 of an inch = 0.0001 in.</p><h3>Install</h3><p>Install the crankshaft thrust bearing support to the cylinder block.</p><p>Tighten the six capscrews and lock washers in the sequence shown in the illustration.</p><div class=\"callout tip\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">🔧</span>Момент затяжки · Torque Value</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>60 n•m [44 ft-lb]</p></div></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01400689.png\" alt=\"01400689.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>This component or assembly weighs greater than 23 kg [50 lb]. To prevent serious personal injury, be sure to have assistance or use appropriate lifting equipment lift this component or assembly.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Complete the upper main bearing installation prior to installing the crankshaft. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-001-006-tr\">Refer to Procedure 001-006 in Section 1.</a></p></div></div><p>Use a lint-free cloth. Clean the crankshaft bearing journals.</p><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>The end of the crankshaft with the gear that has the smallest diameter <b>must</b> point toward the front of the block.</p></div></div><p>Install the crankshaft.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ks6shha.png\" alt=\"ks6shha.png\"></figure><h3>Finishing Steps</h3><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>This component or assembly weighs greater than 23 kg [50 lb]. To prevent serious personal injury, be sure to have assistance or use appropriate lifting equipment lift this component or assembly.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Batteries can emit explosive gases. To reduce the possibility of personal injury, always ventilate the compartment before servicing the batteries. To reduce the possibility of arcing, remove the negative (-) battery cable first and attach the negative (-) battery cable last.</p></div></div><ul><li>Install the thrust bearings. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-001-007-tr\">Refer to Procedure 001-007 in Section 1.</a></li><li>Install the main bearing caps. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-001-006-tr\">Refer to Procedure 001-006 in Section 1.</a></li><li>Install the cylinder head. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-002-004-tr\">Refer to Procedure 002-004 in Section 2</a>.</li><li>Install the piston and connecting rod assembly. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-001-054-tr\">Refer to Procedure 001-054 in Section 1.</a></li><li>Install the connecting rods. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-001-054-tr\">Refer to Procedure 001-054 in Section 1.</a></li><li>Install the front gear cover. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-001-031-tr\">Refer to Procedure 001-031 in Section 1.</a></li><li>Install the flywheel housing. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-016-006-tr\">Refer to Procedure 016-006 in Section 16.</a></li><li>Install the flywheel, if removed. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-016-005-tr\">Refer to Procedure 016-005 in Section 16.</a></li><li>Install the flexplate, if removed. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-016-004-tr\">Refer to Procedure 016-004 in Section 16.</a></li><li>Remove the engine from the engine stand and install. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-000-002-tr\">Refer to Procedure 000-002 in Section 0.</a></li><li>Remove the thrust bearings. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-001-007-tr\">Refer to Procedure 001-007 in Section 1.</a></li><li>Install the oil pan adapter. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-007-027-tr\">Refer to Procedure 007-027 in Section 7.</a></li><li>Install the oil suction tube. Refer to Procedure 007-035 in Section 7.</li><li>Install new full flow filters. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-007-013-tr\">Refer to Procedure 007-013 in Section 7.</a></li><li>Fill the engine with clean 15W-40 lubricating oil. Refer to Procedure 007-037 in Section 7.</li><li>Connect the air/gas supply to the air starter, if equipped. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/102-012-022\">Refer to Procedure 012-022 in Section 12.</a></li><li>Connect the batteries. See equipment manufacturer service information.</li><li>Operate the engine. Check for leaks.</li></ul>","28-001-019-tr":"<h3>Disassemble</h3><p>Remove the gear <b>only</b> when the crankshaft or the gear is damaged.</p><p>Use a puller jaw, Part Number 3375835, and a bridge assembly, Part Number 3375834.</p><p>The Crankshaft Gear Puller Kit, Part Number 3375840, contains the gear puller jaw and the bridge assemblies required for all Cummins® engine models.</p><p>Install the puller jaw and the bridge assembly.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ks8gema.png\" alt=\"ks8gema.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout warning\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>CAUTION · Осторожно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Do not exceed 475 N•m [350 ft-lb] of torque when turning the puller screw on the puller jaw or the jaw will break.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout warning\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>CAUTION · Осторожно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Do not use a cutting torch. High temperature will damage the gear.</p></div></div><p>Use engine oil. Lubricate the puller screw.</p><p>Heat can be applied to help remove the gear.</p><p>Hold the pry bar steady. Turn the puller screw. Remove the gear.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ks8gemb.png\" alt=\"ks8gemb.png\"></figure><p>Remove the key.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ks8kema.png\" alt=\"ks8kema.png\"></figure><h3>Clean and Inspect for Reuse</h3><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>When using solvents, acids or alkaline materials for cleaning, follow the manufacturer&#x27;s recommendations for use. Wear goggles and protective clothing to reduce the possibility of personal injury.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Wear appropriate eye and face protection when using compressed air. Flying debris and dirt can cause personal injury.</p></div></div><p>Use a hone stone. Polish the outside diameter. Remove any small scratches, burrs, or small grooves.</p><p>Clean with solvent. Dry with compressed air.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ks8kema.png\" alt=\"ks8kema.png\"></figure><p>Measure the outside diameter.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Crankshaft Outside Diameter (Gear Location)</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>mm</td><td></td><td>in</td></tr><tr><td>111.25</td><td>MIN</td><td>4.380</td></tr><tr><td>111.28</td><td>MAX</td><td>4.381</td></tr></tbody></table></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ks800ta.png\" alt=\"ks800ta.png\"></figure><p>Measure the inside diameter</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Front Crankshaft Gear Inside Diameter</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>mm</td><td></td><td>in</td></tr><tr><td>111.165</td><td>MIN</td><td>4.3766</td></tr><tr><td>111.195</td><td>MAX</td><td>4.3778</td></tr></tbody></table></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ks6geta.png\" alt=\"ks6geta.png\"></figure><h3>Assemble</h3><p>Install a new key.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ks8keha.png\" alt=\"ks8keha.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>To reduce the possibility of severe burns, wear protective gloves when installing the heated crankshaft gear.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout warning\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>CAUTION · Осторожно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>When heating the crankshaft gear, do not exceed the specified time or temperature. Damage to the crankshaft gear and to the crankshaft gear teeth will result.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout warning\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>CAUTION · Осторожно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Do not attempt to install the crankshaft gear without heat. Damage to the crankshaft gear and to the crankshaft gear teeth will result.</p></div></div><p>Use an oven. Adjust the temperature to 232° C [450° F]. Heat the gear in the oven for a minimum of 1 hour to a maximum of 6 hours. The inside diameter of the gear will become larger and will simplify the installation of the gear on the crankshaft.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/dp8gewa.png\" alt=\"dp8gewa.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>To reduce the possibility of severe burns, wear protective gloves when installing the heated crankshaft gear.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout warning\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>CAUTION · Осторожно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Allow the air to cool the gear. Do not use water or oil to reduce the cooling time. It can cause the gear to crack.</p></div></div><p>Use Lubriplate® Number105, or equivalent. Lubricate the outside diameter of the crankshaft at the gear location.</p><p>Remove the gear from the oven.</p><p>The timing marks on the crankshaft gear <b>must</b> be visible from the front of the crankshaft after the gear is installed.</p><p>The keyway <b>must</b> be aligned with the key in the gear. Install the gear.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ks8geha.png\" alt=\"ks8geha.png\"></figure>"};
